+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // JET ROUND (P2) -- ORDER-KEY RANK TERMS. Ranking only: not one acceptance threshold, claim
+    // tolerance, braid fraction or count is touched by any of them, and every one is INERT AT 0,
+    // which reproduces the frozen key bit for bit (that is the A/B, not an approximation).
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // The K9 claim is the primary selector in a jet core, not a tie-breaker: it keeps 6.69% of
+    // gate-alive chains there against 39.4% at PU200, and 43% of the whole jet-core efficiency loss
+    // is its doing (FINDINGS_JETPHYS.md). It ordered on `score - orderAlpha*hinge(marginX)` alone,
+    // and measured over the pairs that actually contend for hits (a candidate is rejected as soon
+    // as 3 of its hits are owned, so contention means >= 3 shared hit rows), the two ingredients of
+    // that key rank a core-TRUE chain above a core-FAKE one with AUC .717 (marginX) and .464
+    // (score) -- the second is worse than a coin flip, because `score`'s edge-logit content comes
+    // from a head applied where 1 edge in 1,270 is true instead of 1 in 8. Each core-true candidate
+    // must beat a MEDIAN OF 7 fake contenders, so a per-duel rate near .5 is fatal.
+    //
+    // These weights add per-chain purity evidence the key was missing, from columns K7 has already
+    // written into ChainsSoA::features when K9 runs -- zero new compute, no retrained head:
+    //   features[20] minT3FakeScore   AUC .884   features[21] maxT3FakeScore  AUC .838
+    //   features[2]  sumEdgeLogit     AUC .449 (so its contribution is worth REMOVING or inverting)
+    // A dcaXY rank term separates better still (AUC .886) and was REFUSED after measurement: it buys
+    // jet core +.078 and costs the PU200 dxy[0.1,1) band -.031 with PU200 fakes +8.2%, i.e. it is
+    // paid for out of the displaced advantage. See [P2] in standalone/FINDINGS_JETROUND.md.
+    //
+    // *** THE TWO T3 FAKE-SCORE WEIGHTS ARE NOT DISPLACED-NEUTRAL ON THEIR OWN. *** An earlier
+    // version of this comment claimed they were, on the grounds that they read LST's FAKE head and
+    // not its prompt head. That was an argument, not a measurement, and the measurement refuted it:
+    // with these two weights at 200 / 80 and no credit, the displaced efficiency falls on two
+    // independent PU200 samples (dxy[1,5) -.0143 at p 2.2e-08, vxy[10,30) -.0105 at p 1.9e-06).
+    // Use them WITH orderT3DispCredit below, and read that block before choosing a value.
+    //
+    // Shape: a penalty, never a bonus, so a clean chain's key is EXACTLY its old value:
+    //   orderKey -= orderT3FakeMinW * max(0, clamp01(features[20]) - credit_min)
+    //   orderKey -= orderT3FakeMaxW * max(0, clamp01(features[21]) - credit_max)
+    //   orderKey -= orderEdgeLogitW * max(0, features[2])
+    // The clamp is what makes a NaN or an out-of-range logit unable to poison the rank comparator
+    // (the two T3 scores are sigmoid outputs and were measured strictly inside (0,1) over 287k
+    // chains on both samples, so the clamp never fires; it is a guard, not a working point).
+    float orderT3FakeMinW = 0.f;
+    float orderT3FakeMaxW = 0.f;
+    float orderEdgeLogitW = 0.f;
+    // C3, the DISPLACED CREDIT on those two legs, and the reason it exists.
+    //   credit_min = orderT3DispCredit * max(features[22], features[23])
+    //   credit_max = orderT3DispCredit * max(features[22], features[24])
+    // The three T3 scores are ONE SOFTMAX (`Triplet.h:129-131`: fake = t3Scores[0], prompt = [1],
+    // displaced = [2]), so they are not independent: a genuinely displaced T3 has a low PROMPT score
+    // and that probability mass has to go somewhere, which puts it at a systematically HIGHER
+    // fakeScore than a prompt-true T3 sits at. LST itself compensates for exactly this with a working
+    // point 5-15x looser for displaced than for prompt ON THE SAME OUTPUT
+    // (`interface/alpaka/Common.h:73-78`: 0.4957 prompt vs 0.0334 displaced in the first pt bin), so
+    // an uncredited weight on fakeScore is a PROMPT-CALIBRATED TAX and it lands on real displaced
+    // tracks. Measured: displaced-true chains pay 49.4 key units against prompt-true chains' 11.5,
+    // on a fake-vs-prompt margin of 50.9 -- i.e. three quarters of the way to being treated as fakes.
+    //
+    // The credit column is max(prompt, displaced) rather than displaced alone, deliberately: it is
+    // the same construction the chain gate's own axis uses (marginX = max(zPrompt, zDisp) - zFake)
